WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:06.940
OK, so Arizona's a very interesting state
since the passing of the late John McCain.

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:10.630
Martha McSally was appointed as senator there.

00:00:10.630 --> 00:00:15.460
She has never won a campaign, but she's running
to keep her seat in November.

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:19.929
She's running against or running against her,
I think is better the right way to say it

00:00:19.929 --> 00:00:21.960
is Democratic challenger Mark Kelly.

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:25.300
Mark Kelly is a former astronaut, twin brother
of Scott Kelly, who's been a guest on this

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:28.060
program in a new poll out of Arizona.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:33.050
Mark Kelly is ahead of Martha McSally by 13
points.

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:37.980
On an average in recent polling, Mark Kelly
is ahead by nine points.

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:41.280
Now let's look at prior results in Arizona.

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:47.649
In 2012, Republican Jeff Flake defeated the
Democratic challenger, Richard Carmona by

00:00:47.649 --> 00:00:50.289
three in 2016.

00:00:50.289 --> 00:00:56.879
John McCain won in Arizona by 13, although
admittedly that the numbers are complicated

00:00:56.879 --> 00:00:58.949
by the fact that there was a three way race.

00:00:58.949 --> 00:01:03.769
The Green Party candidate got about 6 percent
and presumably most of that 6 percent came

00:01:03.769 --> 00:01:05.070
from the Democratic candidate.

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:09.180
So McCain won by 13, could have been more
like seven.

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:14.210
Had there been no Green Party candidate right
now, the Republican incumbent in Arizona is

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:16.340
down by 13 to Mark Kelly.

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:20.740
So what's interesting is to look not just
at the Senate numbers in Arizona, but also

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:25.550
look at the presidential numbers in the same
poll where Martha McSally is down 13 to Mark

00:01:25.550 --> 00:01:26.550
Kelly.

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:32.329
Joe Biden is ahead of Donald Trump by seven
in Arizona, 50 to 43.

00:01:32.329 --> 00:01:35.189
Now, we want to be careful with these numbers.

00:01:35.189 --> 00:01:38.380
50 plus 43 is 93.

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:43.369
That leaves 7 percent either undecided or
third party.

00:01:43.369 --> 00:01:46.930
A lot of that 7 percent could break for Trump.

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:49.130
And then you would have a real race in Arizona.

00:01:49.130 --> 00:01:50.130
But it could also reverse.

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:54.369
Right, if the economy does not improve.

00:01:54.369 --> 00:01:59.130
If the corona virus continues to run rampant,
it could go more in Biden's favor.

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:05.079
At the same time, if November sees us with
a lull in Corona virus, maybe between waves

00:02:05.079 --> 00:02:09.340
one and two, or with an economy that is looking
better, it could end up looking far better

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:10.569
for Donald Trump.

00:02:10.569 --> 00:02:15.819
These are just bad numbers for Trump and Martha
McSally today.

00:02:15.819 --> 00:02:19.310
In 2016, Trump won Arizona by about three
and a half.

00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:24.459
So Biden plus seven is a ten point swing in
the other direction.

00:02:24.459 --> 00:02:27.230
Now, why do we care so much about Arizona?

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:28.230
Arizona?

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:29.510
You know, it's a relatively small state.

00:02:29.510 --> 00:02:31.840
It's only eleven electoral votes.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:34.150
Why does Arizona matter so much?

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:36.390
What you have to understand.

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:42.290
Is that elections rarely go as 50 completely
isolated elections, where what happens in

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:47.489
Arizona has nothing to do with what happens
in Wisconsin or in Michigan, for example,

00:02:47.489 --> 00:02:50.190
if you see a big reversal in one place.

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:55.310
Often there will be a number of other places
where you will see a move in the same direction.

00:02:55.310 --> 00:03:02.209
When you look at Trump's path to victory,
all paths to victory reasonably assume that

00:03:02.209 --> 00:03:06.159
he has to hold Arizona and probably he has
to hold Florida.

00:03:06.159 --> 00:03:10.129
Florida, Florida's a very big question mark
for Donald Trump right now, particularly with

00:03:10.129 --> 00:03:14.220
older voters getting away from Trump and going
in the direction of Joe Biden.

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:19.060
If Trump doesn't get Florida, Arizona is essentially
a must win.

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:23.850
And in just about every scenario, Arizona
becomes a must win in addition to Wisconsin,

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:26.370
Michigan, Pennsylvania, North Carolina.

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:31.450
Trump can win 270 electoral votes without
Arizona, but it's really, really tough.

00:03:31.450 --> 00:03:37.090
And if Trump does lose Arizona, you have to
imagine it's not just that he loses Arizona.

00:03:37.090 --> 00:03:40.560
He probably loses something else from 2016
as well.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:43.970
This is why looking at the Senate race matters
so much.

00:03:43.970 --> 00:03:45.780
Biden plus seven over Trump.

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:46.780
Maybe you believe it.

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:47.840
Maybe you don't.

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:52.440
Mark Kelly plus 13 over incumbent Republican
Senator Martha McSally.

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:59.730
Even if the lead is only half of that, a Democratic
challenger knocking out an incumbent Republican

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:04.650
senator in Arizona by 6 or 7, which is half
of what this last poll says.

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:08.920
That does not bode well for Republicans in
general and it would not bode well for Donald

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:10.580
Trump in Arizona as well.

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:12.310
Really interesting race to look at.

00:04:12.310 --> 00:04:13.680
And we're going to follow it.

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:20.200
In 2018, six out of 10 federal offices in
Arizona went to Democrats.

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:27.000
That could be the turning point year in Arizona
for the red blue balance in that state.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:33.420
And, of course, Mark Kelly is no progressive
hero, but he is so much better than Martha

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:34.420
McSally.

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:37.890
And it would be a big win in terms of just
their politics as well.

